  },
  {
    q: 'How old are the houses in Briarwood?',
    a: 'It depends which part, and the range is nearly thirty years. The City of Midland recorded the first Briarwood section in July 1984, surveyed by Max W. Richardson, and further sections followed through 1986, 1988, the 1990s, a busy run in the early 2000s, and on to a twenty-first section in November 2012. So a house here might sit on a slab poured in the mid-1980s or one poured after 2010. That matters because older slabs have had decades of settling, plumbing repairs and re-landscaping around the perimeter, with any original termite pre-treatment long past being a factor, while newer ones sit on more recently disturbed fill with a much shorter history.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Am I definitely in the Briarwood Homeowners Association?',
    a: 'Worth checking rather than assuming, and there is a genuine puzzle here we could not resolve. The association states it covers 259 homes and that the first homes were built in 1988. The city\'s plat record, though, shows the first Briarwood section recorded in 1984 and eighteen sections in total running to 2012. Two hundred and fifty-nine homes across eighteen platted sections is a low number, which suggests the association may cover only part of the platted Briarwood area rather than all of it. We are flagging that rather than guessing at an answer. If it matters to you — and for pool access, assessments and covenant obligations it does — your deed and the recorded documents will say. The covenants are recorded in Midland County at Volume 1191, with amendments at Volume 815 and Volume 1459.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do the covenants say anything about my yard?',
    a: 'The recorded documents do address landscaping and property condition, and we are going to be careful about how we put that. We have read the association\'s management certificate, which lists the recorded instruments, but we have not read the full Declaration text itself — and secondary summaries of covenants circulate widely and are not always accurate. So rather than quote you words we cannot stand behind, here is the honest version: yard condition is a covenant matter in Briarwood, unlike some Midland neighbourhoods where it is purely a city code question, and the actual wording is recorded at Volume 1191, Page 1, with amendments. Read your own copy, or ask the association for one, rather than trusting any website\'s paraphrase.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Briarwood in a floodplain?',
    a: 'Not according to the City of Midland\'s published mapping — the plat area centroid does not fall within the mapped 100-year floodplain, and no mapped playa lake sits inside the plat extent. That is a better answer than some north Midland neighbourhoods get; Saddle Club, for instance, has a named playa against its north-east corner and mapped floodplain across part of its Lakes section. Practically it means mosquito pressure in Briarwood is driven by water on properties — pools, irrigation, containers — rather than by a basin nearby that fills after every storm. As always, for anything that actually matters, check your own parcel with the city rather than with a pest control website.',
  },
  {
    q: '¿Atienden Briarwood, en Midland?',
    a: 'Sí, y Midland forma parte de la ruta habitual. Valentin atiende en español y hace el trabajo personalmente. Un dato local que vale la pena: la asociación de Briarwood mantiene dos albercas, y una alberca cuya bomba deja de funcionar se convierte en criadero de mosquitos en cerca de una semana — más que todas las macetas de la cuadra juntas. Si su alberca va a estar apagada varios días en calor, eso ya es un tema de plagas. Llame o mande un texto al (432) 278-7294.',
  },
